.TH SCRINIUM 1 "October 2026" "scrinium 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
scrinium \- notes organised by topic, lockable with a password
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scrinium
[\fB\-\-theme\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-new\fR] [\fB\-\-minimise\fR]
.br
.B scrinium rescue
{\fBlist\fR|\fBopen\fR|\fBunlock\fR} [\fITITLE\fR] [\fB\-\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Scrinium
keeps short notes (numbers, procedures, codes) as cards grouped by topic, automatically saved as plain text files.
Each note can be locked with a password (AES\-256\-GCM, key derived with scrypt). An optional rescue file, created by
the user, can reopen a note whose password was forgotten.
.PP
Without arguments, the main window opens. If Scrinium is already running (possibly hidden in the notification area),
that window is shown instead of starting a second instance.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " NAME"
Open directly on this topic.
.TP
.BR \-\-new ", " \-\-nouvelle
Open with an empty note ready to type.
.TP
.B \-\-minimise
Start hidden in the notification area (useful when started at login).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH RESCUE COMMAND
.TP
.B scrinium rescue list
List locked notes and tell whether they can be reopened with a rescue file.
.TP
.BI "scrinium rescue open " TITLE " \-\-key " FILE
Print the text of a locked note using the rescue file (PEM private key).
.TP
.BI "scrinium rescue unlock " TITLE " \-\-key " FILE
Put a locked note back in plain text.
.PP
The French spellings \fBsecours lister|ouvrir|deverrouiller\fR and \fB\-\-cle\fR are also accepted.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/scrinium/
Data folder on Linux (\fIfiches/\fR: notes, \fIcorbeille/\fR: trash, \fIsettings.json\fR, \fIplugins/\fR: extensions).
.TP
.I ~/.config/scrinium/recovery.pub
Public part of the rescue key. The rescue file itself (private key) is never kept by Scrinium.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SCRINIUM_HOME
Use another data folder.
.TP
.B SCRINIUM_LANG
Force the interface language (\fBen\fR, \fBfr\fR). By default the system language is used.
.SH SEE ALSO
Full documentation: /usr/share/doc/scrinium/ and https://github.com/jfkneib/scrinium
